Bug: refactoring without -p asserts instead of using the discovered project

serverPerformRefactoring (src/refactory.c) asserts options.project != NULL and
copies it into refactoringOptions.project for the inner parseBufferUsingServer
calls. -p is per request (startup.c clears it), and every other operation works
from options.detectedProjectRoot, so a client relying on discovery kills the
server on its first refactoring. Emacs is not affected: it always sends
-p <active project>.

setArguments already leaves out -p when the project is NULL, so the assert may
be all that stands in the way. Check that before choosing between falling back to
the discovered project and reporting an error with errorMessage when there is
neither. An assert is wrong either way: a missing request option is user input.
